\begin{proof}
Let \(H\leq G\) with \([G:H]=p\). Let \(G\) act on the \(p\) left cosets of \(H\), giving a homomorphism \(\varphi:G\to S_p\), and let \(K=\ker\varphi\). Then \(K\leq H\), since an element fixing every left coset in particular fixes \(H\).

Because \(G\) is a \(p\)-group, \(|\varphi(G)|=[G:K]\) is a power of \(p\). Also \(|\varphi(G)|\mid p!\), and the highest power of \(p\) dividing \(p!\) is \(p\). Hence \([G:K]\) is either \(1\) or \(p\).

If \([G:K]=1\), then \(K=G\), so \(H\) is fixed by conjugation and is normal in \(G\). If \([G:K]=p\), then \([G:K]=[G:H][H:K]=p[H:K]\), so \([H:K]=1\) and \(K=H\). Since \(K\) is the kernel of the coset action, \(K\trianglelefteq G\), and therefore \(H\trianglelefteq G\).
\end{proof}
